[{:seon.task/name "web-server-threads-are-joined-at-stop"
  :seon.task/plan "When the web proc binds its port, ring-chez-adapter's :fibers strategy runs its acceptor and its connection sweeper as two futures (two OS threads of Jolt's solo pool, named clojure-agent-send-off-pool-N as on the JVM; the adapter's `start-sweeper!` and its acceptors in src/ring_chez/adapter.clj). The acceptor polls accept() in accept-poll-ms slices; the sweeper is (Thread/sleep 100) in a loop, a clock (docs/clocks.md#if-nothing-has-changed-we-do-nothing). stop-server waits for the acceptor but only flags the sweeper, so after `seon.system/stop!` the sweeper outlives its owner for up to 100 ms: root's thread count after stop! depends on timing, so bin/check lifecycle runs with SEON_WEB_PORT=0. The adapter's :keep-alive-timeout-ms 600000 and :write-timeout-ms 60000 (`seon.web/web-proc`) are application timers and literals (docs/clocks.md#time-decides-owed-its-owners-fix). Correct: the listener is a fiber parked on the listen fd's readiness (jolt.io-poller); an idle connection parks on its own read (a fact, not a sweep); a stuck write is the peer's half-open socket, the kernel's keepalive (the owner's exception); the web proc's ::flow/stop returns after both are gone. Then `seon.check/declared-threads` drops its \"clojure-agent-send-off-pool\" 2 for a listening web proc, and lifecycle runs with the web on. Deletes: start-sweeper!, the accept poll slice, the two adapter timeouts, the SEON_WEB_PORT=0 in bin/check's lifecycle step. A dependency change: on a jolt/<topic> branch of the adapter. Read 2026-11-06: `seon.web/web-proc` still passes the two timeouts, and `seon.check/declared-threads` still declares \"clojure-agent-send-off-pool\" 2 (named \"clojure-agent-pool\" before Jolt 601c9379). The proof named below is green on main with that declaration, so it shows the change only once that line goes."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.system-test/root-threads-are-what-root-declares}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-tabs-first-keyframe-settles-on-its-own"
  :seon.task/plan "Two symptoms of bin/check idle waiting with no end, one occurrence each so far. (1) 2026-11-08: the tab's first keyframe and settled mark never came (below). (2) 2026-11-10: the tab was past its keyframe and both settled marks, after the check's spawn, and waited for the bytes its last change should send. The check's commit ({:ns/name idle.check}) is nothing root's page reads: root's pull covers no namespace entity, root's own writes send no event (so root's process tile, the writer's commit count, is not read again), and seon.render-test/a-commit-root-does-not-show-owes-its-tab-nothing shows such a commit sends the tab nothing. So commit-reached-tab ended only on bytes something else happened to send (the new agent's status moving), and under load those landed before its count was read. Fixed in the check (Web, 2026-11-10): it commits a held task, which root's head counts (seon.render-test/a-commit-root-shows-reaches-a-quiet-root-tab: the tab is sent a patch). seon.render-test/a-spawn-reaches-a-quiet-root-tab shows the spawn wait is owed too, and seon.render.proc/process! already marks :root dirty when root's process value moves. Evidence for (2): ps at the checkpoint puts the agent's fork about 4 s after boot (the check's own spawn, after the two settled waits and the 2,000 ms window), lsof shows the check's feed socket open; the fiber dumps (all parked, none naming its proc) are in the managing session's notes folder, idle-hang-2026-11-10/. bin/check idle now prints \"IDLE waiting: <name>\" before each of its five waits, so the next occurrence names its wait. Symptom (1)'s cause stays open: read from that line when it recurs. Noted, not for this task: root's process value is a second path into root's ratom beside its pull (the owner's option a), so a number in it that moves with no event (the writer's commits on root's own writes) shows stale; that is decided at stage 3/4.

Seen 2026-11-08 in bin/check idle (a land of jolt/rootagent with main's af549ba258 and f9bda5bd88 merged): the check's tab opened the root view's feed and waited about 30 minutes for its \": settled\" mark; the check's root was idle (every fiber parked on core.async alts, no CPU). A second request to the same feed (curl /feed?view=root) got its keyframe at once, and within seconds the check's own tab settled and the step ended IDLE OK. So a tab's first keyframe or settled mark can wait on another tab opening the same view: the web proc's open of a tab (seon.web http-in :open: :views to the render proc, then :keyframe) or the render proc's answer to it loses a keyframe asked while another is in flight, or answers only a view's newest tab. Evidence: the Web session's scratchpad idle-hang-40858 (fiber dump, sample, lsof) and the run's target/check output. Find the cause before fixing. Read so far (Web, 2026-11-08): a tab is stale until a keyframe reaches it (seon.web keyframe!, to every stale tab of its view), and a settled mark goes only to fresh tabs (offer-all skips stale ones), so a tab whose keyframe never comes never settles: the second tab's keyframe reached the check's stale tab too, which is why it unstuck. The render proc answers a keyframe at once when the view is ready? (rendered; a branch read), else holds it in :kf-wanted and asks root's state, sending it from the pass an answer brings; a pass runs only on an input, so a wanted keyframe whose answer's pass still finds its view not ready, with nothing else in flight, waits for ever. Ruled out: a nil keyframe (view-html composes a string skeleton; compose refuses nil by contract). Candidate cause, read 2026-11-08 night (seon.render.proc answered): an error answer to :state (or to :main) is taken and nothing more (\"the next event asks again\"), so a keyframe in :kf-wanted or a page in :waiting waits for the next event, which an idle root never has: a wait whose producer answered an error, with nothing to end it (every wait ends, with its value or a typed error: docs/errors.md#nothing-crashes-and-nothing-hangs). Not shown to be this hang's: the check's log has no defect line for it. Fixed on jolt/findings (seon.render.proc answered; proof seon.render-test/a-wanted-keyframe-is-answered-when-its-read-fails, red then green): an error answer to :state or :main holds each waiting view's page as the error, drawn, which answers its keyframe and page and walks again at every pass until a read draws it. If the hang recurs with no error answer, the cause is elsewhere: read the pass after :state's answer below. The class fix as planned was: on an error answer, every page waiting and keyframe wanted is answered with that error drawn (a page :status 503 by the error's render; a keyframe that draws it, so the tab turns fresh and the next commit redraws it), and the proof is a keyframe asked while :state answers an error is answered. Next otherwise: the pass after :state's answer for the root view (root-page is nil until main is read: is main's read asked, and does its answer's pass send :kf-wanted?), and keyframe! closing a tab whose offer! is refused.

Proofs, red first: a tab that opens a view while that view's keyframe for an earlier tab is in flight gets its own keyframe and settled mark, with no other tab opening. Seen again 2026-11-10 09:00-09:15 (Optimization): jolt/config-entity's bin/land prove, check run 20261110T083842-5614 at load 8-10 beside two other sessions' proves: the idle step waited 35 minutes at 0% CPU (root 16253, its fork server, standby and agent parked), ended by pid (exit 143, red verdict kept); alone on the same tree green in 7.8 s; earlier idle runs took up to 89 s (target/check/20261106T094051). The branch did not touch seon.render.proc, seon.web or seon.check/idle. Evidence (fiber dumps, sample, lsof, thread lists of the four processes): the managing session's notes folder, ~/Library/Application Support/Claude/side-session-notes/local_a7c56472-459f-4556-894b-f463b25767c4/idle-hang-2026-11-10/ (kept while this task is open). jolt/findings' fix (an error answer holds the waiting view's page) is on main since d859c84d5b, so it does not cover this occurrence: the cause is still open."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.web-test/each-tab-gets-its-own-keyframe}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-request-to-a-proc-whose-step-never-answers-ends"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: web (reviewed by flow). The class: a request to a proc whose step never answers. What exists [M, read jolt/flow at 2026-11-14 21:50]: seon.web/ask! (web.clj:128) derefs (step/ask-edge ch m nil) with no end, the edge's blocking read for a page or keyframe; the render proc answers it from its step. A step that throws, or (with Jolt Flow's :step-ticks) ends over its budget, takes Flow's step-error path: its outputs dropped, its message seen only by the error chan, so the asker waits for good: nothing hangs (docs/errors.md#nothing-crashes-and-nothing-hangs) is broken, budget or not. Cure at the source (flow's design, deps building it in Jolt): on a :step-ticks end the proc's next step gets cid :clojure.core.async.flow/step-ended with {:msg :cid :ticks-spent :ticks-budget}, and the render proc answers that request with its typed error drawn (as seon.render-test's a-wanted-keyframe-is-answered-when-its-read-fails does for a failed read); a step that throws is answered the same way through the api's existing defect answer (seon.system/answer-taken) or its render equivalent. No bound at the edge: the answer comes from where it failed. Proof, red first: a :page request whose render step ends over budget is answered with its typed error. Needs Jolt's step-ended (deps' pin after next, ~03:30). Fallback, only if Jolt's step-ended cannot land (web-2, 2026-11-14): ask! derefs with a declared :seon.config.web/answer-ms and answers :seon.bound/reached, drawn; it is a deadline on an internal wait, which rules 3 and 4 of docs/flow.md#the-five-rules push against, so it is the second choice and goes when step-ended lands. Duplicate seon.web/a-page-request-is-answered-whatever-its-step-does was cancelled in its favour. The budget fn reads render's max-turns-held, an O(1) read of its state (web-2 builds it with this)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.web-test/a-page-whose-render-step-ends-is-answered-with-its-error}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-page-change-needs-no-rebuild"
  :seon.task/plan "Root's ruling (2026-11-15): resources/public/ stops being a build input (:seon.config.build/inputs, default.edn:412). Today input.css is compiled by Tailwind in bin/build (build_css, bin/build:232) into target/css/app.css, and datastar.js and highlight.js are build inputs too, so a page change there forces a rebuild and a restart (bin/binary-digest). Change: the stylesheet's compile becomes a runtime effect keyed by input.css's digest and the src classes it scans (the existing effects owner, a :css effect beside :embed), its output served from the tree or the store; the .js files served from the tree. Extends: seon.web's static serving, bin/build's css step (deleted), seon.fx's effects. Priority: after the pages reader land (switch-over item 2). Proof: a change to input.css changes no binary digest and the page serves the new stylesheet after its effect runs. Cites: `seon.web/handler`, `docs/render.md#tailwind`."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.web-test/a-stylesheet-change-changes-no-binary}
  :seon.task/estimate #:seon.web{handler {:n 1, :order :constant, :ticks 50000, :bytes 100000}}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-system-seen-from-flow"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: web (designs and builds; the owner asks web to improve this design and to impress him); flow reviews; the owner, 2026-11-16: a view of the system from Flow's perspective, a port of core.async's flow-monitor mapped into Seon's way, built on the best base, with no new system: it waits for the reactive core (seon.ratom/web-reads-on-the-core) and deletes the broken parallel designs it replaces.\nWHAT FLOW-MONITOR DOES (upstream, the JVM): serves a page of one flow: its topology (procs and conns), each proc's status and state (its ping), each channel's buffer fill, the report and error streams, and controls (pause, resume, inject a message). It samples ping on a timer and pushes over a WebSocket.\nSEON'S WAY: (1) the topology is data already: resources/seon/system.edn read by seon.graph/read-decl, plus the agents' procs the api joins at each update!; (2) nothing is sampled on a clock: what moves is pushed as it happens, from the sources that exist: the events mult (system.clj:1846, tapped at :1983), Flow's report and error chans (piped to the api's :back), the writer's commit notices on :main, and a proc's ping-map only when asked (``seon.system/stall-state``, system.clj:304, one Flow ping); (3) the page is a render over reactions (seon.ratom), whose sources are those channels and the store, so a proc's row redraws only when its own values move; (4) one stream to every reader: a browser's page over /feed (web.clj:249, SSE, the channel the web proc feeds) and a Claude session's Monitor tool over the server's line stream (seon.server/*emit*, server.clj:541, as :prove's stream=true writes) are two wire paths for one job: keep one, or show why both; (5) messages are its first consumer: seon_inbox stream=true since=<t>, the existing op improved (seon.server/a-message-is-read-by-value-and-marks-nothing), is a stream of the agent's message notices, the read a query since t, nothing marked; no new op or tool is added for any of this (the owner: use and improve the tools Seon agents use).\nINTERFACE AGREED with flow (2026-11-16; reference upstream flow-monitor at reference-code/core.async.flow-monitor 421d56c, its server flow_monitor.clj): (1) topology once a graph, (datafy graph): the writer process's read in place, an agent's by a transport op {:op :flow-graph}; re-read when update! changes a graph. (2) a snapshot on demand (a page opened, a proc expanded), one ping a graph as Jolt answers it (status, count, ::flow/site with :since-ns, ::flow/in-fill per in-port, ::flow/unanswered and ::flow/report-dropped as meta; jolt flow/impl.clj:391-434), an agent's by {:op :flow-ping}; never on a loop. (3) moves pushed: (a) each proc's status transition, one {::flow/pid ::flow/from ::flow/to} on the report chan (a Jolt change, asked of deps); (b) a put that parks on a full chan reported once, and once when it clears (Jolt, asked of deps), the fast path paying nothing; (c) a step past a bound is NOT pushed (it would need a timer, the stall detector the owner deleted): the snapshot shows a long step's site and :since-ns when looked at; (d) the report and error chans as a mult the api's :back and the page each tap (flow, at the graph's start). (4) controls (pause, resume, inject) after the read-only view, through existing ops.\nRESTART: a stream ends with root and is reopened with since=<t>; what it showed is facts, so nothing is lost and nothing repaired; at a switch-over its last line is :seon.system/restarting with :next-port.\nPARALLEL DESIGNS TO DELETE, each with what does its job (web to confirm by reading): the two wire streams (4); the notice paths that each carry part of one fact's moves (the events mult, the reconciler's :wake list on message-write, the api's ::waits preds over commits); stall-state's evidence gathered by a ping only at a hang, beside a view that shows procs live.\nCOST: lower bound one push per moved value per open reader; idle zero; no timer.\nPROOFS: web names them, red first, in its design; the owner judges the view in a browser."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/estimate #:seon.web{handler {:n 1, :order :constant, :ticks 20000, :bytes 20000}}
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.ratom/web-reads-on-the-core"}}]
